===Etymology===
Attested since 1835<ref name="Dict">{{R:Dictionary.com}}</ref><ref>{{R:MWO}}</ref> as ''Kanuk'' (in US writings) and 1849 as ''canuck'' (in Canadian writings). {{unk|en}} origin.
{{rel-top|hypotheses}}
Several dictionaries hypothesize that it may be a simple alteration of {{m|en|Canada}}.<ref>{{R:ODO}}</ref><ref name="AHD">{{R:AHD}}</ref> More than one hypothesis holds that the name began as an informal self-appellation by an early Canadian minority, and later acquired a national identity. A few sources explain the ending as coming from {{cog|iu|inuk||man, person}}, from {{m|en|Chinook||Aboriginal people of the U.S. Pacific Northwest}},<ref>{{R:Etymonline}}</ref> or another Canadian Aboriginal ending like {{m|und||-uc}}, {{m|und||-uq}}, or {{m|und||-oc}}.

Another hypothesis is that the name is from the surname {{m|fr|Connaught}} {{IPAchar|/ˈkɑ.nəxt/}}, used as a French-Canadian nickname for the Irish. Yet another speculates that the origin is {{cog|lre|kanata||village}}, which is also the origin of {{m|en|Canada}}. It has also been thought to come from Iroquoian {{m|und|Canuchasa||hut}}, {{cog|de||[[Genug]] von Canada|enough of Canada}}, or {{cog|fr||[[quelle]] [[canule]]}}.

Since 1975, a number of scholars have come to believe that the name may come from {{cog|haw|kanaka||man}},<ref name="Dict"/><ref name="AHD"/><ref>{{R:Collins English Dictionary}}</ref> a self-appellation of indentured colonial [[canoeman|canoemen]] and Hawaiian sailors working off the Pacific Northwest, Arctic, and New England coasts. The term may have come to English through {{cog|fr|canaque}}, or more likely via American [[whalers]], and then later been re-interpreted<ref name="Dict"/> as {{m|en|Canadian|Can(adian)}} + a suffix. Compare English {{m|en|Kanak}} and French {{m|fr||canaque|black person}}, German {{m|de|Kanake}}.
{{rel-bottom}}

